1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,280
Whether you memorize a script or read from one,

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:12,560
or use a few notes, or don't use any

3
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:15,610
notes at all, a talk that is

4
00:00:15,610 --> 00:00:18,660
well prepared and passionately delivered will always impact

5
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:19,660
an audience.

6
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:24,400
But what does delivering a talk passionately look like?

7
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:28,280
It's time to find your voice.

8
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:29,720
Here's a radical question.

9
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:32,000
Why bother to give a talk?

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,090
Why not simply email the script

11
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:36,180
to every potential member of the audience?

12
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:39,170
One of the special things about TED is

13
00:00:39,170 --> 00:00:42,160
that it shows how talks can offer something more

14
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:44,200
than printed words.

15
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,879
But that doesn't just happen automatically.

16
00:00:46,879 --> 00:00:50,500
Not every talk offers something extra.

17
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:53,290
That something extra has to be

18
00:00:53,290 --> 00:00:56,080
thought about, invested in, and developed.

19
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:58,200
It has to be earned.

20
00:00:58,200 --> 00:00:59,599
What is that something extra?

21
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:02,420
It's our humanity.

22
00:01:02,420 --> 00:01:07,160
Our humanity turns information into inspiration.

23
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:09,500
Here are some of the things the

24
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,840
human layer can bring to a text.

25
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:15,160
I trust this person.

26
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:18,840
Every sentence sounds so interesting.

27
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:21,420
I hear it in your voice

28
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:24,000
and see it in your face.

29
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The emphasis on that word with that hand gesture?

30
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,800
Now I get it.

31
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:31,800
Empathy.

32
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:34,820
I can tell how much that hurt you.

33
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:35,820
Excitement.

34
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:39,180
Wow, that passion is infectious.

35
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:40,400
Conviction.

36
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:42,820
Such determination in those eyes.

37
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:43,820
Action.

38
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:46,539
I want to be on your team.

39
00:01:46,539 --> 00:01:48,539
Sign me up.

40
00:01:48,539 --> 00:01:52,860
Together, these things create inspiration.

41
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:55,270
We think of inspiration as the force that tells

42
00:01:55,270 --> 00:01:57,680
the brain what to do with a new idea.

43
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:58,680
Many of us are inspired by the idea of inspiration.

44
00:01:58,680 --> 00:01:58,780
We think of inspiration as the force that tells

45
00:01:58,780 --> 00:01:58,880
the brain what to do with a new idea.

46
00:01:58,880 --> 00:01:58,960
Many of us are inspired by the idea of inspiration.

47
00:01:58,960 --> 00:01:58,980
Many of us are inspired by the idea of inspiration.

48
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,480
Many ideas just get filed away and probably soon forgotten.

49
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:06,380
Inspiration, on the other hand, sets

50
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:09,280
off our mind's attention alert system.

51
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:11,620
There are two major things to consider

52
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:13,960
when preparing a talk for an audience.

53
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:18,020
The first is, what am I doing with my voice?

54
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:20,260
And the second is, what

55
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:22,500
am I doing with my body?

56
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:24,280
Speak with meaning.

57
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:26,580
Listen to the opening minute of

58
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:28,880
the TED Talk by George Monbiot.

59
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:31,210
When I was a young man, I spent six

60
00:02:31,210 --> 00:02:33,540
years of wild adventure in the tropics working as

61
00:02:33,540 --> 00:02:36,140
an investigative journalist in some of

62
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:38,740
the most bewitching part of the world.

63
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:41,030
I was as reckless and foolish as only young

64
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:43,320
men can be, this is why wars get fought.

65
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:45,570
But I also felt more

66
00:02:45,570 --> 00:02:47,820
alive than I've ever done since.

67
00:02:47,820 --> 00:02:51,060
And when I came home, I found the

68
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:54,300
scope of my existence gradually diminishing until loading

69
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:56,940
the dishwasher seemed like an interesting challenge.

70
00:02:56,940 --> 00:02:58,760
And I found that I have power.

71
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:00,350
I found myself sort of

72
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:01,940
scratching at the walls of life,

73
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:04,000
as if I was trying to find a way out

74
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,260
into a wider space beyond.

75
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:10,260
I was, I believe, ecologically bored.

76
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:12,420
When you hear Monbiot speak,

77
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:15,680
you find yourself pulled into his world.

78
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:18,220
He uses his voice in a way that adds meaning

79
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:20,359
to every word he says.

80
00:03:20,359 --> 00:03:21,500
That's something you wouldn't get

81
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:23,440
if you read his script in a book.

82
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:25,380
His words are interesting,

83
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:27,739
but it's the way he says them

84
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:29,260
that gives his audience a feeling

85
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:31,900
of curiosity and astonishment.

86
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:33,060
How does he do this?

87
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,100
The key to being an exciting speaker

88
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:40,420
is to create variety in the way you speak.

89
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:44,560
Variety based on the meaning you're trying to get across.

90
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:46,580
So many speakers forget this.

91
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:48,470
They give a talk in

92
00:03:48,470 --> 00:03:50,360
which every sentence sounds the same,

93
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:52,500
with a slight rise at the

94
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:54,640
start and a drop at the end.

95
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:57,280
There are no pauses or changes of pace.

96
00:03:57,280 --> 00:03:59,920
This communicates that no single part of a talk

97
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:01,980
matters more than any other part.

98
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:04,090
It's just the same thing over

99
00:04:04,090 --> 00:04:06,200
and over again until the end.

100
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:08,240
Listening to a talk like

101
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:10,280
this can feel like being hypnotized.

102
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:13,320
That is, it puts an audience to sleep.

103
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:16,120
If your talk is scripted, try this.

104
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:18,140
Find the most important two

105
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:20,160
or three words in each sentence

106
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:21,640
and underline them.

107
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:25,120
Then look for the one word in each paragraph

108
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:26,600
that really matters,

109
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:27,040
and underline them.

110
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:28,920
Underline it twice.

111
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:32,000
If there are bits that are lighthearted or playful,

112
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,480
run a wavy pencil line under those.

113
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:36,720
Look for every question mark and highlight it

114
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:38,560
with a yellow highlighter.

115
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:43,000
Find the single biggest aha moment in the whole talk

116
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,800
and put a great big black blob right before it.

117
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:48,940
If there's a joke or a funny story,

118
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:51,340
put little pink dots above it.

119
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:53,720
Now, try reading your script, making

120
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:56,600
sure to change your voice in response to each mark.

121
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:00,640
For example, let yourself laugh a little

122
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:02,800
while looking at the pink dots.

123
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:06,320
Pause for the big black blob.

124
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:09,520
And speed up a bit for the wavy pencil line.

125
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:12,120
Or speak more softly.

126
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:13,800
How does that sound?

127
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:16,080
Now try one more thing.

128
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:19,520
Remember all the emotions associated

129
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:22,160
with each section of your talk.

130
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:25,160
Which bits are you most passionate about?

131
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:26,920
Which issues make you angry?

132
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:29,240
What makes you laugh?

133
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:31,360
What confuses you?

134
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:34,720
Read your talk again, letting those emotions out

135
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:37,320
a little as you speak.

136
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:39,120
Try doing this with a friend.

137
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:41,000
See what she responds to or

138
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:42,880
what she rolls her eyes at.

139
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:44,880
Record yourself reading your talk,

140
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:47,380
then play it back with your eyes closed.

141
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:49,240
How's it sounding?

142
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:52,120
Don't struggle to add variety to your voice

143
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:54,000
when that doesn't feel right to you.

144
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,920
Let it come naturally from the passion you feel

145
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:58,560
for your idea.

146
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:01,840
You want to sound like yourself, expressing curiosity

147
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:04,320
and excitement when it's appropriate.

148
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:06,480
You know the voice you use when you're catching up

149
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:08,060
with friends or family members

150
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:09,640
you haven't seen in a while?

151
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:11,800
That's the kind of voice you'll want to use

152
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:13,440
to present your talk.

153
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:17,360
Real, natural, and unafraid to let it rip

154
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:19,840
if what you're saying demands it.

155
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:22,640
It's important to use your body, too.

156
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:24,720
Sometimes, a speaker will give the impression

157
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:26,280
that his body is only there to carry his thoughts.

158
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:29,159
It's important to carry his head onto the stage.

159
00:06:29,159 --> 00:06:30,599
Once in front of the

160
00:06:30,599 --> 00:06:32,039
audience, the body no longer knows

161
00:06:32,039 --> 00:06:33,359
what to do with itself.

162
00:06:33,359 --> 00:06:36,799
It stands awkwardly, hands glued to its side,

163
00:06:36,799 --> 00:06:39,499
or sways back and forth

164
00:06:39,499 --> 00:06:42,200
from one leg to the other.

165
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:44,400
But there are a few things you can think about

166
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:47,799
that may help you feel more comfortable on stage

167
00:06:47,799 --> 00:06:51,359
and make you seem more confident to your audience.

168
00:06:51,359 --> 00:06:54,159
The simplest way to present a talk powerfully

169
00:06:54,159 --> 00:06:56,039
is just to stand tall.

170
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:58,840
Put equal weight on both your feet,

171
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:01,039
keeping your feet a few inches apart.

172
00:07:01,039 --> 00:07:04,119
Use your hands and arms to naturally emphasize

173
00:07:04,119 --> 00:07:05,840
whatever it is you're saying.

174
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:07,779
This is enough to signal

175
00:07:07,779 --> 00:07:09,719
to the audience that you're calm

176
00:07:09,719 --> 00:07:12,320
and that you know what you're talking about.

177
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:14,140
This is the method used

178
00:07:14,140 --> 00:07:15,960
by a majority of TED speakers.

179
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:18,320
The key is to feel relaxed

180
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:21,280
and to let your upper body move freely.

181
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:23,159
Good posture helps.

182
00:07:23,159 --> 00:07:25,760
Avoid slouching your shoulders forward.

183
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:29,520
Standing so openly may feel vulnerable,

184
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:31,839
but that vulnerability can be

185
00:07:31,839 --> 00:07:34,159
nice for an audience to see.

186
00:07:34,159 --> 00:07:37,159
Some speakers prefer to walk the stage.

187
00:07:37,159 --> 00:07:38,520
It helps them think.

188
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:40,599
It helps them emphasize key moments,

189
00:07:40,599 --> 00:07:42,280
and this is important,

190
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:46,119
they frequently stop to dwell on important points.

191
00:07:46,119 --> 00:07:47,979
It's that change in rhythm

192
00:07:47,979 --> 00:07:49,840
that makes the walking method work.

193
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:52,640
Constant pacing without a change in rhythm

194
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:54,719
can be tiring to watch.

195
00:07:54,719 --> 00:07:55,279
But pacing, broken up, and relaxed,

196
00:07:55,279 --> 00:07:55,840
can be a great way to relax.

197
00:07:56,280 --> 00:08:00,200
But by moments of stillness can be powerful.

198
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:04,440
Something to avoid is nervously shifting from leg to leg.

199
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:07,679
Or walking forward and back a couple of steps

200
00:08:07,679 --> 00:08:10,520
in a kind of rocking motion.

201
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:13,520
Many speakers do this without realizing it.

202
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:15,479
They may be feeling a little anxious

203
00:08:15,479 --> 00:08:17,199
and shifting from one leg

204
00:08:17,199 --> 00:08:18,919
to the other eases their discomfort.

205
00:08:18,919 --> 00:08:20,880
But from the audience's point of view,

206
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:24,039
it actually highlights that discomfort.

207
00:08:24,039 --> 00:08:26,239
The difference between a speaker who rocks

208
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:29,719
back and forth and a speaker who finds stillness

209
00:08:29,719 --> 00:08:32,360
can be huge for an audience.

210
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:34,360
So move if you want to,

211
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:38,240
but only move if you can move with purpose.

212
00:08:38,240 --> 00:08:40,400
Then when you want to emphasize a point,

213
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:42,959
stop moving and address your audience

214
00:08:42,959 --> 00:08:46,480
from a stance of calm power.

215
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:48,080
There are plenty of other ways

216
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:49,680
you can speak with power too.

217
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:53,280
Dame Stephanie Shirley chose to sit for her talk,

218
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:53,780
using a metal stool with one

219
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:54,280
foot tucked back on a run,

220
00:08:54,280 --> 00:08:54,780
using a metal stool with one

221
00:08:54,780 --> 00:08:55,280
foot tucked back on a run,

222
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:55,760
using a metal stool with one

223
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:56,240
foot tucked back on a run,

224
00:08:56,280 --> 00:08:58,600
and notes in her lap.

225
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:01,319
It looked relaxed and natural.

226
00:09:01,319 --> 00:09:03,600
The late great neurologist Oliver Sacks

227
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:05,680
also sat for his talk.

228
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:08,640
Clifford Stoll leapt and darted around the stage

229
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:11,680
with such energy it added an entirely new

230
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:14,400
and unique flavor to his talk.

231
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:16,199
So there are no rules.

232
00:09:16,199 --> 00:09:17,439
You just have to find

233
00:09:17,439 --> 00:09:18,680
a way of being on stage

234
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:22,240
that makes you feel comfortable and confident,

235
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:25,640
and that doesn't distract from what you're saying.

236
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:27,400
The simple test is to rehearse

237
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:29,160
in front of a small audience

238
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:31,000
and ask them if your body language

239
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,520
is getting in the way of your message.

240
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:37,000
Or record yourself speaking and watch it back

241
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:38,319
to see if you're doing anything

242
00:09:38,319 --> 00:09:40,760
you didn't know you were doing with your body.

243
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:41,920
There's a place in the world

244
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:44,520
for all types of presentation styles.

245
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:46,319
Just make sure your body knows

246
00:09:46,319 --> 00:09:49,240
it's not just there to carry your head around.

247
00:09:49,240 --> 00:09:52,960
It's allowed to enjoy its time on stage.

248
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:54,720
Do it your way.

249
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:56,360
Don't try to be someone else.

250
00:09:56,360 --> 00:10:00,160
Focus on your idea and your passion for it.

251
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:03,180
And don't be afraid to

252
00:10:03,180 --> 00:10:06,200
let your own personality shine through.

253
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:07,680
This is one of the first

254
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:09,160
steps in forming a trusting bond

255
00:10:09,160 --> 00:10:12,760
with your audience, which is exactly what we focus on

256
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:14,280
in our next video.

257
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:16,120
Check it out.

258
00:10:17,762 --> 00:10:23,583
ご視聴ありがとうございました

